1. what is Data Hiding?
   Data hiding means restricting direct access to a class's internal data by declaring fields as private and providing controlled access through methods such as getters and setters
   Ex:-
   class Employee {
    private String name;
    private double salary;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
   }

2. what is encapsulation?
    Encapsulation is an OOP principle where we wrap the data and the methods that operate on that data into a single unit. It also provides controlled access to the internal data by using access modifiers like private and getter/setter methods.

3.Advantages and disadvantages of Encapsulation?
  advantages:-
  a) data hiding
  b) controlled access
  c) Data validation
  d) Maintainability
  e) Reduce coupling
  f) security

  disadvantages:-
   a) more boiler plate code and slows down execution
   b) slight complex
   c) possible unnecessary  exposure

4) what is tightly encapsulated class ?
   A class is said to be tightly encapsulated if and only if each and every instance variable of the class is declared as private. Whether the class contains corresponding getter and setter methods or not, and whether those methods are declared as public or not, we don't need to consider these things.

5) what is inheritance ?
   Inheritance is an OOPS principle where a new class acquires the properties and behaviors of an existing class using the extends keyword. The main advantage of inheritance is code reusability.

6) Has-A relationship or Association?
   HAS-A relationship represents a relationship where one class contains a reference to an instance of another class. It is used to achieve code reusability through composition or aggregation rather than inheritance.

   Note: Easy memory
   IS-A → Inheritance → extends
   HAS-A → Composition/Aggregation → object reference

7) what is composition ?
   Composition is a strong relationship between two classes. If the contained object cannot exist independently without the container object, then the relationship is called composition. It represents a strong HAS-A relationship and results in tight coupling between the objects.
   Ex:-
   class Engine {
   }

   class Car {
    private Engine engine = new Engine();
   }

8) what is Aggregation ?
   Aggregation is a weak HAS-A relationship between two classes where the contained object can exist independently of the container object.

   class Employee {
   }

   class Department {
    private Employee employee;

    Department(Employee employee) {
        this.employee = employee;
    }
   }

9)what is method signature?
  A method signature in Java consists of the method name and the parameter list. The parameter list includes the number, type, and order of parameters. The return type is not part of the method signature.
  ⭐ Easy memory
      Method Signature = Method Name + Number + Type + Order of Parameters
  The compiler uses the method signature to resolve overloaded method calls at compile time.
  with in a class 2 mehods with same signature not allowed.

10)what is Overloading ?
   Method overloading is a feature of Java where, within the same class, we define multiple methods with the same name but different parameter lists. The parameter list can differ in number, type, or order of parameters.
   ⭐ In method overloading, method resolution is performed by the compiler at compile time based on the method signature. Therefore, method overloading is called compile-time polymorphism, static polymorphism, or early binding.

   a) Automoatic promotion in overloading-- while resolving the overloaded methods if the exact method is not available then we don't get any compiletime error. it will promote the argument to the next level and check wether the matched is available or not.
     byte->short
	            ->int->long->float->double
     char------
   b) while resolving overloaded methods compiler will always give the more prescedence to child type argument when compared with parent type argument
   c)When overloaded methods have two unrelated reference types at the same level, passing null can result in an ambiguous method call because null is compatible with both types and neither type is more specific than the other.
   Ex:-
   void show(String s) {
    System.out.println("String");
   }

    void show(StringBuilder sb) {
    System.out.println("StringBuilder");
    }

	show(null);
    Both methods are applicable because null can be assigned to any reference type:
    null → String
    null → StringBuilder
	➡️ Compile-time error: reference to show is ambiguous.

	d) If overloaded methods have parameters in different orders and we pass arguments that can be promoted to match both methods equally, the compiler cannot determine which method to invoke, resulting in an ambiguous method call.
	Ex:-
	m1(int i, float f)
	m1(float f , int i)

	m1(10, 10.5f)--int float
	m1(10.5f, 10)-- foat int
	ma(10,10)-- ambiguous compiletime error

	e) var arg method will get the least presendence if no exact match is availbal then givve a chance to the var-arg.
	Varargs methods have the lowest priority during method overloading. The compiler first tries to find an exact match or an applicable method using normal method invocation rules. Only if no applicable fixed-arity method is found, the compiler considers the varargs method.

