15) what is Overring?
    Method overriding is a feature of Java where a child class provides its own implementation of a method that is already defined in the parent class. It requires an inheritance relationship between the parent and child classes.
	In method overriding, the method implementation is determined at runtime based on the actual object, so it is called runtime polymorphism or dynamic polymorphism.

16) Rules for overriding
    a) method signature must be same and return type is not part of the method signature. and covariant return types are allowed.
	Ex:-
    Same method signature       ✅
    Return type can be subtype   ✅
    Completely different return type ❌
	b) we can't over ride private methods.
	c) A final method cannot be overridden by a child class. A final class cannot be extended, so its methods cannot be overridden either.
	d) An abstract method must be implemented (overridden) by the concrete child class. If the child class does not implement all inherited abstract methods, then the child class must also be declared abstract.
	e) In method overriding, the modifiers strictfp, native, and synchronized do not affect the method's overriding rules. The child method can have these modifiers independently, subject to the normal overriding rules.
	f)we can't decrease the scope of modifiers (private<default<protected<public)
	g)If the parent class method throws a checked exception, the child class overriding method can throw the same checked exception, a subclass of that exception, or no exception. It cannot throw a broader checked exception.
	h) we can't override static methods. even if we did that is method hiding not a overriding.
	i) A varargs method can be overridden by another method with the same varargs signature. Since varargs is internally treated as an array, int... and int[] have the same signature. If the child defines int instead of int..., it is a different method and therefore is not overriding.
	j) Method overriding is applicable only to methods, not variables.

17) what is polymorphism?
    Polymorphism means "one thing having multiple forms." In Java, the same interface or parent reference can represent different objects, and the behavior can change based on the actual object.

18) what is coupling?
    Coupling is the degree of dependency between two classes or components. It indicates how strongly one component depends on another.
	Types
    Tight coupling → strong dependency; changes in one component can require changes in another.
    Loose coupling → minimal dependency; components can change independently.

